<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madurai Nayak dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a>–<a href="/years/1736">1736</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nayaks of Madurai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> Nayak dynasty ruled much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> from 1529 to 1736. Its founders were governors for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> who became independent after Vijayanagara's defeat in 1565, ruling from Madurai and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>. Thirumalai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> (r. 1623–1659) is remembered for his palace and for additions to the Meenakshi temple. The line ended in 1736, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> overthrew Queen Meenakshi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> were Telugu-speaking Balija warriors from the Rayalaseema region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. Nagama Nayaka, a general of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a>, was sent to reassert Vijayanagara authority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> territories, but his strict rule alienated the local chieftains, and in the late 1520s he began to defy the capital. The emperor sent his son Viswanatha Nayaka, who defeated his father and sent him under guard to the capital, where Nagama was pardoned. Viswanatha was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1529. A traditional chronicle tells of Nagama usurping the Pandyan throne instead, but it lacks contemporary inscriptions to support it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Viswanatha rebuilt the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and abdicated in 1564 in favour of Krishnappa Nayaka I, whose troops sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in 1565 arrived too late; the defeat of Rama Raya left the southern Nayak kingdoms practically autonomous. Muttu Krishnappa Nayaka recognised Sadeika Thevar as Sethupathi at Ramanathapuram in 1604, and Muttu Virappa Nayaka I backed Gobburi Jagga Raya in the Vijayanagara civil war, lost at the Battle of Toppur in 1616, and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>. Tirumala Nayaka took it back to Madurai in 1635, enlarged the army to about 30,000 troops and stopped all payments to the empire.</p><p>Chokkanatha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in 1673, but in 1675 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekoji-i">Venkoji</a> took it and founded the Thanjavur Maratha kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-mangammal">Rani Mangammal</a>, regent from 1689 to 1704, paid tribute to the advancing Mughal army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfiqar-khan">Zulfiqar Khan</a> and helped it at the siege of Jinji. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-ranga-chokkanatha">Vijaya Ranga Chokkanatha</a> died without issue in 1732, his widow and the claimant Bangaru Tirumala fought over the succession, and the queen turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a>, who was acting for the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Viswanatha and his dalavay, Ariyanatha Mudaliar, divided the kingdom into 72 palayams, each held by a hereditary palayakkarar, anglicised as polygar, who kept armed retainers and judged local cases. A chief sent one third of his revenue to the treasury, kept one third for his troops and retained the rest. The court used Telugu, while most of the people spoke Tamil. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> enlarged the Meenakshi-Sundaraswarar temple with enclosures, pillared halls and gateway towers up to 50 metres high, and a related line ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> from 1739 to 1815.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madurai Nayak dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mysore's early armies were raised from local chiefs and peasant infantry, and the Wodeyars fought frequent wars with neighbouring chiefs, the <b>Nayakas of Madurai</b> and Keladi, and the Bijapur Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
